 "futan-gendogaku-nintei": {
  "name": "Reduced Food and Accommodation Costs at Care Facilities (Burden Limit Certification)",
  "short": "Lower food and accommodation costs for facility stays and short stays",
  "target": "Among people admitted to long-term care insurance facilities (special nursing homes for the elderly, long-term care health facilities, and long-term care medical facilities) or using short stays, those who meet both the income requirement (household exempt from residence tax, receiving public assistance, etc.) and the asset requirement on savings and other assets may be eligible.",
  "benefit": "With a burden limit certification from the municipality, food and accommodation (stay) costs are reduced to a limit set by income stage, and the difference is covered by long-term care insurance as a benefit for designated facility residents. The daily food cost limits for facility admission (after the August 2026 revision) are 300 yen for Stage 1 (people receiving public assistance, etc.), 390 yen for Stage 2, 680 yen for Stage 3-①, and 1,420 yen for Stage 3-② (the standard cost amount is 1,545 yen). Food cost limits for short stays are set separately (from August 2026, 1,030 yen for Stage 3-① and 1,360 yen for Stage 3-②). Accommodation costs are also reduced to limits based on room type and income stage (for example, under the standards effective August 2026, 530 yen per day for Stage 3-② in a multi-bed room; amounts for conventional private rooms and other room types vary by facility type).",
  "window": "Municipal long-term care insurance office (市区町村の介護保険担当窓口)",
  "documents": [
   "Long-term care insurance burden limit certification application form",
   "Consent form (for inquiries into savings and other assets)",
   "Copies of the bankbooks of the person and their spouse (the parts showing account details and recent transactions; in the example of Kita City, Tokyo, the most recent 2 months)",
   "A document verifying the My Number (detailed requirements vary by municipality)"
  ],
  "steps": [
   "Before facility admission or a short stay, apply for burden limit certification at the municipal long-term care insurance office",
   "Declare the savings and other assets of the person and their spouse with bankbook copies or similar documents",
   "The income stage (Stage 1 to Stage 3-②) is determined, and those who qualify are issued a burden limit certificate",
   "Presenting the certificate to the facility reduces food and accommodation costs to the limit amounts",
   "The certificate is valid until July 31, so people continuing to use it complete renewal procedures every August"
  ],
  "notes": [
   "Income stages are divided by the amount of pension and similar income within households exempt from residence tax; from August 2026, the boundary between Stage 2 and Stage 3-① became 826,500 yen in pension and similar income (809,000 yen before the revision)",
   "Approximate asset requirements for a single person are 10 million yen or less for Stage 1, 6.5 million yen or less for Stage 2, 5.5 million yen or less for Stage 3-①, and 5 million yen or less for Stage 3-② (add 10 million yen to each when there is a spouse; confirmed in August 2026)",
   "Households subject to residence tax are in principle not eligible, but a special reduction measure exists for cases such as when facility admission would make it hard for a spouse remaining at home to make a living",
   "Accommodation cost limits are set in detail for each facility type and room type, so the exact amounts need to be confirmed with the municipality or the facility"
  ],
  "recent": "In August 2024, the standard amounts for accommodation costs and related items were revised. Furthermore, from August 1, 2026, the standard daily food cost was raised from 1,445 yen to 1,545 yen, the food cost limits for Stages 3-① and 3-② were raised (to 680 yen and 1,420 yen), the accommodation cost limit for Stage 3-② was raised, and the income stage threshold was revised (from 809,000 yen to 826,500 yen)."
 },
 "kaigo-kyugyo-kyufu": {
  "name": "Family Care Leave and Care Leave Benefits",
  "short": "Leave to care for a family member, with benefits worth 67% of wages",
  "target": "Family care leave can be taken by workers caring for an eligible family member (spouse (including de facto marriage), parents, children, spouse's parents, grandparents, siblings, or grandchildren) who is in a condition requiring constant care for 2 weeks or more. The care leave benefit may be available to employment insurance insured persons who, in the 2 years before the leave began, have 12 or more months with at least 11 days counted for wage payment (or at least 80 hours worked).",
  "benefit": "Family care leave can be taken for up to 93 days in total per eligible family member, split into up to 3 periods. The care leave benefit pays \"wage daily amount at the start of leave × number of benefit days × 67%\" (requirements include that 80% or more of wages are not being paid during the leave). Benefits are capped; the amounts listed on Hello Work Internet Service (as confirmed on 2026-08-29) are a monthly wage cap of 546,600 yen and a monthly benefit cap of 366,222 yen (caps are revised every year on August 1).",
  "window": "Family care leave is requested from the employer. Benefit applications are made, in principle through the employer, to the Hello Work office (ハローワーク) with jurisdiction over the workplace.",
  "documents": [
   "Family care leave request form (employer's format)",
   "Care leave benefit application form (in most cases the employer handles the procedure)",
   "Documents confirming the relationship to the family member and the fact of caregiving, wage ledger, attendance records, and the like (as instructed by the employer or Hello Work)"
  ],
  "steps": [
   "Request family care leave from the employer (by law, the request should in principle be made at least 2 weeks before the planned start date)",
   "Take the leave (up to 93 days in total per eligible family member, divisible into up to 3 periods)",
   "After the leave ends, apply for the care leave benefit at the Hello Work office with jurisdiction, in principle through the employer",
   "Submit by the application deadline (the last day of the month that includes the date 2 months after the day following the end date of each care leave (or, when a care leave has passed 3 months, the day 3 months after the leave start date))",
   "After the payment decision, the benefit is transferred"
  ],
  "notes": [
   "Separate from family care leave, there is also short-term care leave for accompanying hospital visits, handling procedures, and the like (5 days per year for 1 eligible family member, 10 days per year for 2 or more; can be taken in hourly units)",
   "The care leave benefit has further requirements, such as working 10 days or fewer in each benefit unit period",
   "Employers are also expected to provide work-care balance measures, such as limits on overtime work and shorter working hours for caregiving, which can be checked at the same time"
  ],
  "recent": "Under the revised Child Care and Family Care Leave Act effective April 1, 2025, employers are required to individually inform workers who face family caregiving about support systems and confirm their intentions, provide information early to workers around age 40, and put in place training, consultation services, and other workplace measures. The provision allowing labor-management agreements to exclude workers with less than 6 months of service from short-term care leave was abolished, and introducing telework for caregiving periods became a best-effort obligation for employers."
 },
